-- allium: 3
-- cli.allium
--
-- Scope: The `dispatch` command line as a whole — which subcommands exist,
--        how a process run as one of them ends (its exit code), how the
--        one-shot commands reach the shared store, and what the commands that
--        no other spec owns read and print.
-- Includes: The subcommand inventory and where each is specified; the
--           exit-code contract (success, failure, usage error, and the one
--           code that blocks a gated tool call); the three process classes a
--           subcommand runs in; how one-shot commands open the store
--           (CliCommandsReachTheStoreWithoutManagingIt), including the
--           store address a board leaves beside its database, and the host
--           file they need and never write (CliCommandsNeedAHostFile); the
--           operator-facing
--           output of `repo set-verify`, `repo clear-verify`, `repo list`,
--           `prune-repo-paths`, `plan`, `spacetime dump-server|restore` and
--           `store import`; the plan file check that precedes the store;
--           and the terminal hand-back every companion-pane renderer owes
--           its shell.
-- Excludes: `dispatch tui` and everything a launch does before the board
--           draws (see startup.allium); the caller identity headers and
--           `dispatch caller-headers`' one answer (see mcp-task-tools.allium:
--           TheHelperAnswersSessionUnconditionally, and dispatch.allium:
--           AgentCarriesItsOwnCallerIdentity); the `hook`, `hook-subagent` and
--           `hook-peer-message` subcommands (see agent-health.allium:
--           HookDelivery); `pr-gate`'s verdict and fail-open (see
--           pr-workflow.allium: PrLearningsGate); `statusline` (see
--           observability.allium: StatusLineDecorator); `verify-feed` (see
--           feeds.allium: VerifyFeed); what `agent-tree`, `agent-diff` and
--           `toggle-agent-tree-pane` show and how they wait for their task
--           (see agent-tree.allium, "Companion Pane Startup"); what
--           `repo status` and `repo sync` measure and do (see repo-sync.allium:
--           RepoStatusCli, RepoSyncCli); what the snapshot commands and the
--           import write into a store and when they refuse (see
--           spacetime-seed.allium: SnapshotCommandLine, ImportOldStore; startup.allium: ImportBringsUpTheManagedStore);
--           the plan attachment itself (see mcp-task-tools.allium:
--           AttachPlanViaCli); `uninstall` (see mcp-task-tools.allium, "Other
--           CLI subcommands"); where a one-shot command's log lines go (see
--           observability.allium).

use "./core.allium" as core

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- How a `dispatch` process ends, as seen by whatever ran it.
--
-- success: exit 0. The command did what it was asked, or (for the commands
--   whose contract is never to fail — statusline, caller-headers,
--   toggle-agent-tree-pane) it ran at all.
-- failure: exit 1. The command reported an error on stderr, naming what went
--   wrong. Every error a subcommand returns ends this way.
-- usage_error: exit 2, before the command body runs. The arguments did not
--   parse — an unknown subcommand, a missing argument, a value of the wrong
--   kind. The usage text goes to stderr.
-- block_tool_call: exit 2, from `pr-gate` only. Claude Code reads exit 2
--   from a PreToolUse hook as "block this tool call", and every other
--   non-zero code as "report and allow"; see pr-workflow.allium:
--   PrLearningsGate.
enum ProcessExit {
    success
    failure
    usage_error
    block_tool_call
}

-- What machinery a subcommand's process starts before its body runs. Chosen
-- by the subcommand alone, before anything else happens.
--
-- synchronous: no asynchronous runtime at all. statusline, caller-headers,
--   verify-feed, uninstall and toggle-agent-tree-pane. The first two run at a
--   rate (several times a second per session; every MCP session start) where
--   setting up machinery they cannot use is pure waste — see
--   observability.allium: StatusLineDecorator's StartsNoAsyncRuntime.
-- hook: a single-threaded runtime with I/O and timers only. hook,
--   hook-subagent, hook-peer-message and pr-gate: the commands Claude Code
--   runs once per tool call of every live session, whose whole body is one
--   round trip to the running board (agent-health.allium: HookDelivery).
-- general: the full multi-threaded runtime. Everything else, including any
--   subcommand added later — correct by default, merely unoptimised.
enum ProcessClass {
    synchronous
    hook
    general
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- One run of the `dispatch` binary as a subcommand.
entity CliInvocation {
    subcommand: String
    process_class: ProcessClass
    named_store: String?          -- --spacetime-server, else DISPATCH_SPACETIME_SERVER; blank counts as absent
    board_store: String?          -- DISPATCH_BOARD_STORE: the address the board that started this
                                  -- process's tmux session published; blank counts as absent
    recorded_store: String?       -- the address a board on this --data-dir recorded beside it
                                  -- (startup.allium: StoreAddressRecord); blank counts as absent
    exit: ProcessExit?            -- null while the process is still running
}

-- A companion-pane renderer (`agent-tree` or `agent-diff`) and the terminal
-- it borrows from its tmux pane.
entity PaneRenderer {
    invocation: CliInvocation
    task_id: Integer              -- the only argument it is given
    terminal_taken: Boolean       -- raw mode and the alternate screen are on
    status: running | ended

    transitions status {
        running -> ended
        terminal: ended
    }

    -- A renderer that has ended has handed its terminal back, whichever way
    -- its process then exits.
    invariant EndedRendererHoldsNoTerminal {
        status = ended implies not terminal_taken
    }
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- The address one-shot commands reach when no store is named. Same value
    -- as startup.allium's config.managed_store_address.
    default_store_address: String = "http://127.0.0.1:3000"
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: reaching the store
------------------------------------------------------------

rule CliCommandsReachTheStoreWithoutManagingIt {
    -- Every subcommand that reads or writes shared rows itself — repo,
    -- prune-repo-paths, plan — opens the store the same way. `store import`
    -- connects the same way once its target is chosen, but chooses it as
    -- spacetime-snapshot.allium's
    -- ImportTargetsTheNamedStoreOrBringsUpTheManagedOne says (see the open
    -- question on whether it reads the recorded address). The companion
    -- panes do not open the store at all (PaneRenderersAskTheBoard, below).
    when: CliStoreRequested(invocation)

    requires: host_file_present()

    let address =
        if invocation.named_store != null: invocation.named_store
        else if invocation.board_store != null: invocation.board_store
        else if invocation.recorded_store != null: invocation.recorded_store
        else: config.default_store_address

    ensures: CliConnectedTo(invocation: invocation, server: address)

    @guidance
        -- The connection is the board's first-connection path: this
        -- install's identity is read from the host file (host.allium:
        -- IdentityLivesInHostFile) — no database is opened
        -- (storage.allium: StoreInUseNeverOpensSqlite) — the store is
        -- connected and the identity settled, and the first subscription
        -- applied. A store that cannot be reached ends the command with
        -- failure, naming the reason (startup.allium:
        -- AbortWhenTheStoreCannotBeReached). That includes a recorded address
        -- whose board has gone without removing it: the command fails naming
        -- that address, and does not fall back to the managed address.
        --
        -- WHERE THE ADDRESS COMES FROM, first answer wins: the flag, the
        -- operator's environment variable (DISPATCH_SPACETIME_SERVER), the
        -- address the board published on its tmux session
        -- (DISPATCH_BOARD_STORE; startup.allium: PublishTheBoardsStoreOnItsSession),
        -- the address a board on the same --data-dir recorded beside it
        -- (startup.allium: RecordTheNamedStoreOnceItAnswers), and last the
        -- managed store's address. The board's variable reaches the
        -- processes started inside the board's own tmux session; the record
        -- reaches everything else, such as a plain terminal.
        --
        -- TWO VARIABLES, TWO OWNERS (task #28729). DISPATCH_SPACETIME_SERVER
        -- is the operator's: only they set it, and `dispatch tui` reads it.
        -- DISPATCH_BOARD_STORE is the board's: only a board sets it, and only
        -- these commands read it -- `dispatch tui` never does, so an address
        -- an earlier board left on the session cannot read as a store the
        -- operator named. A board on the managed store records
        -- nothing and clears any stale record
        -- (ForgetAStaleStoreRecordOnAManagedLaunch), so with it running the
        -- answer falls through to the managed address, where its store is.
        -- The record is read, never written, by these commands.
        --
        -- WHAT IT DOES NOT DO. A short-lived command is not a board: it does
        -- not start, adopt or stop the managed store (only `dispatch tui` and
        -- `store import` manage one), and it does not register this host in
        -- the shared host registry — the board already does that on every
        -- connect (sync.allium: RegisterHostOnConnect). So with no board
        -- running and no store named, these commands fail rather than bring
        -- a store up.
        --
        -- `dump-server` and `restore` do not come through here: they talk to
        -- the server they are given through the `spacetime` CLI
        -- (spacetime-seed.allium). There is no `spacetime dump` (of a local
        -- database) or `spacetime seed` any more (spacetime-seed.allium:
        -- "Seeding (removed)").
        --
        -- == Black boxes ==
        -- host_file_present() asks whether host.allium's HostFile exists in
        -- the data directory.
}

rule CliCommandsNeedAHostFile {
    -- A one-shot command that needs this install's identity, run where there
    -- is no host file yet.
    when: CliStoreRequested(invocation)

    requires: not host_file_present()

    ensures: CliFailed(invocation: invocation, reason: no_host_identity_message())

    @guidance
        -- The command ends with failure, saying that this machine has no
        -- identity yet and that one interactive `dispatch tui` creates it. It
        -- does NOT mint an identity: minting is the board launch's
        -- (host.allium: MintHostIdentity), which is also where the host
        -- label is asked for. Before task #16755 these commands minted one
        -- themselves; that was dropped so that the only writer of a new
        -- identity is the launch that names the machine.
        --
        -- A one-shot command never writes the host file at all, on this path
        -- or any other: renaming the host, adopting an identity and storing
        -- its credential are the board's.
        --
        -- == Black boxes ==
        -- no_host_identity_message() is the line described above.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: commands no other spec owns the output of
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The repo-path commands are operator equivalents of the MCP verify-command
-- tool (mcp-task-tools.allium: "Other CLI subcommands"). What follows is only
-- what they print. Every path the operator types is tilde-expanded before use,
-- the same expansion saved repo paths are stored under.

rule SetVerifyCommandViaCli {
    when: CliSetVerify(path, command)

    ensures: VerifyCommandStored(path: expand_tilde(path), command: command)
    ensures: CliPrinted(line: "verify_command set for " + expand_tilde(path))

    @guidance
        -- `dispatch repo set-verify <path> <command>`. Creates the saved path
        -- when it does not exist yet. A command containing a newline or
        -- carriage return is refused (core/SavedRepoPath's
        -- VerifyCommandSingleLine) and the command ends with failure.
}

rule ClearVerifyCommandViaCli {
    when: CliClearVerify(path)

    ensures: VerifyCommandStored(path: expand_tilde(path), command: null)
    ensures: CliPrinted(line: "verify_command cleared for " + expand_tilde(path))
}

rule ListReposViaCli {
    when: CliListRepos(operator)

    ensures:
        if core/SavedRepoPaths.count = 0:
            CliPrinted(line: "No repo paths configured.")
        else:
            for repo in core/SavedRepoPaths:
                CliPrinted(line: repo_list_row(repo))

    @guidance
        -- `dispatch repo list`. One line per saved path: the path alone, or
        -- the path, a tab and `verify: <command>` when it has one. An empty
        -- list is not an error — the command still succeeds.
        --
        -- == Black boxes ==
        -- repo_list_row(repo) is the line described above.
}

rule PruneRepoPathsViaCli {
    when: CliPruneRepoPaths(operator)

    let missing = core/SavedRepoPaths where not path_exists_on_disk(path)

    ensures:
        for repo in missing:
            SavedRepoPathForgotten(repo: repo)
            CliPrinted(line: "removed: " + repo.path)
    ensures: CliPrinted(line: prune_summary(missing.count, core/SavedRepoPaths.count - missing.count))

    @guidance
        -- `dispatch prune-repo-paths`. Existence is judged on THIS machine's
        -- filesystem, after tilde expansion. The summary line reads
        -- `<removed> path(s) removed, <kept> kept.`
        --
        -- == Black boxes ==
        -- path_exists_on_disk(path) asks the local filesystem;
        -- prune_summary(removed, kept) is the summary line above.
}

rule PlanFileIsCheckedBeforeTheStore {
    -- `dispatch plan <id> <path>`. The file is resolved first; the store is
    -- opened only once it has been.
    when: CliAttachPlanRequested(task_id, path)

    requires: path_exists_on_disk(path)
    requires: canonical_path(path) != null

    ensures: CliStoreRequested(invocation: current_invocation())
    ensures: CliAttachPlan(task: task_by_id(task_id), path: canonical_path(path))

    @guidance
        -- A mistyped path fails at once with "Plan file not found: <path>",
        -- without a store round trip; a path that exists but cannot be
        -- resolved fails with "Failed to resolve plan path …". Either ends
        -- with failure.
        --
        -- The path recorded on the task is the CANONICAL one, not the one
        -- typed: absolute and free of symlinks, so it stays valid from the
        -- agent's worktree, wherever the operator typed it from.
        --
        -- The attachment itself is mcp-task-tools.allium: AttachPlanViaCli.
        -- It goes through the task service, like every other task mutation.
        -- On success it prints `Plan attached to task #<id>: <path>`; a task
        -- id with no task ends with failure and `Task <id> not found`.
        --
        -- == Black boxes ==
        -- current_invocation() is the running CliInvocation; task_by_id(id)
        -- is the task with that id, null when there is none;
        -- path_exists_on_disk(path) asks the local filesystem; and
        -- canonical_path(path) is the absolute, symlink-free path, null when
        -- it cannot be resolved.
}

rule SnapshotCommandsReportWhatTheyDid {
    when: CliSnapshotCommandFinished(command, rows, tables, destination)

    ensures:
        if command = dump_to_file:
            CliReported(on: stderr, line: "Wrote " + destination)
        else if command = restore:
            CliPrinted(line: restored_summary(rows, tables))

    @guidance
        -- The operator-facing lines of spacetime-seed.allium's
        -- SnapshotCommandLine; what each command writes, and when it refuses,
        -- is specified there.
        --
        -- `dump-server` writes the snapshot to the file named by
        -- `--out`, or to stdout for `-` (the default), pretty-printed so that
        -- two backups diff line by line. Only a file destination prints
        -- `Wrote <file>`, and it prints it on stderr — stdout may BE the
        -- snapshot. `restore` reads its file, or stdin for `-`, and prints
        -- `Restored <rows> rows across <tables> tables.`
        --
        -- A refusal is reported as the refusal reason itself, not as a crash,
        -- and ends with failure; by NothingIsWrittenBeforeTheChecksPass the
        -- store was not written to.
        --
        -- == Black boxes ==
        -- restored_summary is the line described above.
}

rule StoreImportReportsItsCounts {
    when: ImportReported(imported, kept, dropped, orphaned)

    ensures: CliPrinted(line: import_summary(imported, kept, dropped))
    ensures: CliPrinted(line: import_detail(imported, kept, dropped, orphaned))

    @guidance
        -- `dispatch store import --from <old store>`. The first line reads
        -- `Imported <n> rows into <server>; <k> were already there, <d>
        -- dropped.`, followed by the per-table detail, which names the
        -- orphaned tasks' ids (spacetime-seed.allium:
        -- OrphansLeaveTheirDroppedEpic).
        --
        -- WHAT `--from` MEANS. An `http://` or `https://` value is a running
        -- store's address; anything else is a data folder. A folder that is
        -- not a directory, or that a running store is serving, is refused
        -- before anything is copied (spacetime-seed.allium: ImportOldStore).
        -- The copy is served for at most 30 seconds' worth of startup before
        -- the import gives up.
        --
        -- With no store named, the `spacetime` CLI must be on PATH for the
        -- managed store to be brought up; without it the command ends with
        -- failure, saying to install it.
        --
        -- == Black boxes ==
        -- import_summary and import_detail are the two parts above.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: the companion-pane renderers
------------------------------------------------------------

rule PaneRendererTakesTheTerminal {
    when: CliPaneRendererStarted(invocation, task_id)

    ensures: PaneRenderer.created(
        invocation: invocation,
        task_id: task_id,
        terminal_taken: true,
        status: running
    )
}

rule PaneRendererEnds {
    -- The user's exit key, or an error from inside the render loop.
    when: CliPaneRendererStopped(renderer)

    requires: renderer.status = running

    ensures: renderer.status = ended
}

rule PaneRendererGivesTheTerminalBack {
    when: renderer: PaneRenderer.status transitions_to ended

    requires: renderer.terminal_taken

    ensures: renderer.terminal_taken = false

    @guidance
        -- `agent-tree` and `agent-diff` take their pane's terminal (raw mode,
        -- the alternate screen, the cursor hidden) for as long as they run.
        -- However the renderer ends — the user's exit key, or an error from
        -- inside the render loop — the terminal is restored before the
        -- process exits. A renderer that left raw mode on would drop the
        -- user into a shell that echoes nothing and shows no cursor, with
        -- nothing on screen to say why; and the failure is identical in both
        -- renderers, so they share the one hand-back rather than each
        -- re-deriving it.
        --
        -- The error, if there was one, is still reported, after the restore:
        -- the process ends with failure.
        --
        -- Each renderer is given only a task id. It reads its task through
        -- the running board (PaneRenderersAskTheBoard), then waits for it as
        -- agent-tree.allium's "Companion Pane Startup" describes.
}

rule PaneRenderersAskTheBoard {
    -- `agent-tree` and `agent-diff` take what they need from the store —
    -- their task, and the live-agent list — from the running board, not
    -- from a connection of their own.
    when: CliPaneRendererStarted(invocation, task_id)

    ensures: CliReadsThroughTheBoard(invocation: invocation)

    @guidance
        -- The board is found the way a hook finds it (agent-health.allium:
        -- HookDelivery): the port named on the command line, else the port
        -- the agent's session was launched with, else the default port. The
        -- panes are not Claude Code processes, so they do not see the port a
        -- hook is handed in the session's settings; instead the board
        -- publishes its port on its own tmux session, beside the store
        -- address it publishes there (startup.allium:
        -- RecordTheNamedStoreOnceItAnswers), and every pane split in one of
        -- its agent windows inherits it. Unlike the store address it is
        -- published whichever store the board runs on, managed or named,
        -- because a board on a non-default port is otherwise unreachable
        -- from its own panes. A board that could not publish it (no session
        -- to publish on) leaves its panes asking the default port, best-effort
        -- like the store address. Being found is not a precondition of
        -- starting: a renderer that cannot reach the board draws in its pane
        -- like one whose task has not arrived, and says so, rather than
        -- ending (agent-tree.allium: AgentTreePaneTaskNeverArrives,
        -- AgentTreeAgentListFailureKeepsLastList).
        --
        -- No store address is resolved for them, so
        -- CliCommandsReachTheStoreWithoutManagingIt's resolution order does
        -- not apply: the board has already resolved it. What they read and
        -- how is agent-tree.allium: BoardPaneView, and the guarantee that
        -- they never touch the store is agent-tree.allium's
        -- PanesReadThroughTheBoard.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

invariant ExitCodeTwoIsReservedForBlockingAndUsage {
    for invocation in CliInvocations:
        invocation.exit = block_tool_call implies invocation.subcommand = "pr-gate"
}

invariant PrGateRunsAsAHook {
    for invocation in CliInvocations:
        invocation.subcommand = "pr-gate" implies invocation.process_class = hook
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Actor Declarations
------------------------------------------------------------

actor Operator {
    identified_by: core/Host where id != ""
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface DispatchCommandLine {
    facing operator: Operator

    exposes:
        for invocation in CliInvocations:
            invocation.subcommand
            invocation.exit

    provides:
        CliSetVerify(path, command)
        CliClearVerify(path)
        CliListRepos(operator)
        CliPruneRepoPaths(operator)
        CliAttachPlanRequested(task_id, path)
        CliPaneRendererStarted(invocation, task_id)
        CliPaneRendererStopped(renderer)

    @guarantee EveryFailureNamesItsCause
        -- A command that cannot do what it was asked ends with a non-zero
        -- exit and says why on stderr, in terms the operator can act on: the
        -- path that does not exist, the task id that has no task, the store
        -- that could not be reached. A script can rely on the exit code; a
        -- person on the message.

    @guarantee OutputIsLineOriented
        -- Every listing prints one line per row, its columns separated by
        -- tabs, with no header (`verify-feed`'s table is the exception, and
        -- feeds.allium's). They are meant to be read by a person and piped
        -- through `cut` or `grep` by a script alike.

    @guarantee NoSubcommandOpensSqlite
        -- No subcommand opens a SQLite database: not `repo`, `plan`,
        -- `prune-repo-paths`, `agent-tree`, `agent-diff`, the hooks,
        -- `uninstall` (whose `--purge` neither reads nor deletes a leftover
        -- tasks.db — mcp-task-tools.allium, "Other CLI subcommands") nor any
        -- other, and the board does not either (storage.allium:
        -- StoreInUseNeverOpensSqlite, CodeNeverTouchesLegacyDatabase).

    @guarantee NoTaskListOrStatusCommand
        -- There is no subcommand that lists tasks or changes a task's status.
        -- The board and the MCP tools are the only surfaces for task state
        -- (mcp-task-tools.allium, "Other CLI subcommands").

    @guidance
        -- SUBCOMMAND INVENTORY, and where each is specified:
        --   tui                         startup.allium
        --   plan <id> <path>            PlanFileIsCheckedBeforeTheStore; mcp-task-tools.allium: AttachPlanViaCli
        --   uninstall [--yes] [--purge] mcp-task-tools.allium, "Other CLI subcommands"
        --   hook, hook-subagent,
        --   hook-peer-message           agent-health.allium: HookDelivery
        --   pr-gate <id>                pr-workflow.allium: PrLearningsGate
        --   agent-tree <task_id>        agent-tree.allium; PaneRendererGivesTheTerminalBack
        --   agent-diff <task_id>        agent-tree.allium; PaneRendererGivesTheTerminalBack
        --   toggle-agent-tree-pane <w>  agent-tree.allium: ToggleAgentTreePane
        --   statusline                  observability.allium: StatusLineDecorator
        --   verify-feed <command>       feeds.allium: VerifyFeed
        --   caller-headers              mcp-task-tools.allium: TheHelperAnswersSessionUnconditionally
        --   repo set-verify|clear-verify|list
        --                               the rules above
        --   repo status|sync            repo-sync.allium: RepoStatusCli, RepoSyncCli
        --   prune-repo-paths            PruneRepoPathsViaCli
        --   spacetime dump-server|restore
        --                               spacetime-seed.allium: SnapshotCommandLine
        --   store import --from <src>   spacetime-seed.allium: ImportOldStore
        --
        -- Two options are global: `--data-dir` (also DISPATCH_DATA_DIR) and
        -- `--spacetime-server` (the store; also DISPATCH_SPACETIME_SERVER, which is
        -- the operator's; the board's own variable is DISPATCH_BOARD_STORE).
        -- `--data-dir` names the data directory itself (storage.allium:
        -- DataDirectory), where the host file is read from and a command's log
        -- lines go. The old `--db` option is gone.
        --
        -- REMOVED with task #16755: `spacetime dump` (a snapshot of the local
        -- database) and `spacetime seed` (an empty store seeded from it). Both
        -- are now unknown subcommands and end with usage_error.
        --
        -- THE COMMANDS THAT NEVER FAIL. `statusline`, `caller-headers` and
        -- `toggle-agent-tree-pane` always end with success, because a
        -- failure from any of them has nowhere useful to go: the first two
        -- would break Claude Code's status line or MCP connection, and the
        -- third runs detached from a tmux key binding. They swallow their own
        -- errors (toggle-agent-tree-pane logs them) rather than return them.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "An unparseable argument list exits 2 before any command body runs, and exit 2 is also Claude Code's 'block this tool call' signal. A `pr-gate` invocation that fails to parse (an id that is not a number, a renamed flag after an upgrade) therefore BLOCKS the gated `gh pr create`, contradicting the gate's fail-open stance (pr-workflow.allium: PrLearningsGate). Is a parse failure meant to block, or should pr-gate map usage errors to a non-blocking code?"

open question "Task #4982: CliCommandsReachTheStoreWithoutManagingIt now falls back to the address a named-store board recorded beside the --data-dir database before the managed address, while `store import` with nothing named brings up the managed store. Should `import` — which would then write into a store some board is running on rather than bring the managed one up — read the recorded address too, or ignore it?"

open question "`prune-repo-paths` judges existence on the local filesystem, but saved repo paths are shared rows in the store (core/SavedRepoPath has no host). Run on one machine, it forgets every repo path that exists only on a teammate's machine. Is pruning meant to be per-host, or is the shared table meant to hold only paths valid everywhere?"
